// Title: Potential Amazon SSM Agent Hijacking
// Sigma ID: d20ee2f4-822c-4827-9e15-41500b1fff10
// Status: test | Level: medium
// Author: Muhammad Faisal
// Date: 2023-08-02 | Modified: -
// ATT&CK: command-and-control, persistence, T1219.002
// Description: Detects potential Amazon SSM agent hijack attempts as outlined in the Mitiga research report.
// False positives:
//   - Legitimate activity of system administrators
// References:
//   - https://www.mitiga.io/blog/mitiga-security-advisory-abusing-the-ssm-agent-as-a-remote-access-trojan
//   - https://www.bleepingcomputer.com/news/security/amazons-aws-ssm-agent-can-be-used-as-post-exploitation-rat-malware/
//   - https://www.helpnetsecurity.com/2023/08/02/aws-instances-attackers-access/
// Sigma rule: https://github.com/SigmaHQ/sigma/blob/05d1031f537e830b76da53bae2400c5814c89165/rules/windows/process_creation/proc_creation_win_ssm_agent_abuse.yml
// License: Detection Rule License (DRL) 1.1 - https://github.com/SigmaHQ/Detection-Rule-License
// Converted by sigma2kql (target: microsoft_xdr, pySigma 1.5.1, pySigma-backend-kusto 1.0.1)
// Validation: passed (Kusto.Language 12.4.1, microsoft_xdr schema)

DeviceProcessEvents
| where FolderPath endswith "\\amazon-ssm-agent.exe" and (ProcessCommandLine contains "-register " and ProcessCommandLine contains "-code " and ProcessCommandLine contains "-id " and ProcessCommandLine contains "-region ")
